There's a list of dirty secrets -

1) Any top level skill takes as much as 10 yeas of
experience.  Programming, master level chess,
plumbing, you name it.  Very few people are willing
to stick with it for 5-10 years before they are even
competitive.  The fact that city colleges churn out
associates degrees in it makes for expectations that
people can program with 2 years of experience and
the market says it just isn't true.  This establishes a
harshly Darwinian process that culls out very many
who enter the field before they reach their prime.
And the entry level jobs aren't the ones that are well
paid.

2) The harshly Darwinian aspect doesn't stop at the
amount of experience, either.  Most people are able
to become a top level professional plumber if they spend
the time acquiring the expertese.  It's a lot of hard work
and hard knocks to learn it, but the mediocre plumbers
are able to perform at maybe a quarter the performance
of the top ones.  Software developement, like chess,
doesn't have anything like that same curve.  The top one
percent programmers produce an astonishing fraction of
the total production in software.  The top ten percent
probably produce more than half of the total.  Be even in
the second ten percent, and you're a part of the thundering
herd.

3) Compare this with medicine, where people know in advance
there's a 12 year learning program.  The second ten percent
among the physicians are the ones you want to go to.  The
top ten percent end so busy they can't give enough face
time.

4) Few in management are willing to accept just how steep
the performance curve is among developers.  So pay is poorly
linked to performance.


